=== daimler-engine
{ "title": "The Grandfather Clock engine", "name": "Daimler “Grandfather Clock” engine", "summary": "The fast-running single cylinder that made a motor vehicle possible: hot-tube ignition and flywheels sealed in the crankcase.", "eyebrow": "Daimler and Maybach single-cylinder engine, 1884–1885", "lede": "Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach's small, fast-running engine, shown turning over in slow motion: the falling piston sucks the intake valve open, a groove in the flywheel pushes the exhaust valve, and a glowing tube lights the mixture near the top of the compression stroke.", "photo": { "alt": "Daimler and Maybach's 1885 Grandfather Clock engine on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen along its crankshaft" }, "components": [ { "label": "Engine", "params": { "label": "Four-stroke cycle · one cylinder" }, "talk": [ "Daimler and Maybach built this engine in Cannstatt in 1884–85. Its upright cylinder on a round crankcase earned it the nickname Standuhr, the Grandfather Clock.", "It ran at about 600 rpm, when most gas engines of the day managed 120 to 180. More revs from the same size means more power for the weight: light enough to put in a vehicle. A version of it drove the 1885 Riding Car.", "There is no spark plug. A tube outside the cylinder is kept glowing by a burner; the compression stroke pushes mixture into it and it lights itself. Watch the glow on the left of the head.", "Only the exhaust valve is driven, by a rod from a curved groove in the flywheel. The intake valve has no cam at all: the falling piston sucks it open." ] } ], "note": "Geometry is illustrative: only the bore and stroke come from period data, and valve and hot-tube positions are simplified.", "unverified": [ "Bore 58 mm × stroke 100 mm (264 cc) and 0.5–1 hp at about 600 rpm: sources disagree, and some figures may describe the Riding Car's version", "Conrod length, valve sizes and timing", "Crankcase size and casing outline, scaled from the photo by assuming a 220 mm case radius", "Room and level" ] }
=== reitwagen
{ "title": "A belt, two pulleys and a ring gear", "name": "Daimler riding car", "summary": "The first vehicle with a petrol engine: half a horsepower, a leather belt, and two gears changed by hand at a standstill.", "eyebrow": "Daimler Reitwagen, 1885", "lede": "Daimler and Maybach put their new fast-running engine into a wooden frame to prove it could move a vehicle. Pick a gear and a hill: half a horsepower goes a long way on the level, and not far uphill.", "photo": { "alt": "Wooden 1885 Daimler riding car with a leather seat on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the right" }, "components": [ { "label": "Belt drive", "params": { "label": "Two-speed belt, pinion and ring gear", "drive": { "gears": [ { "name": "Low" }, { "name": "High" } ] } }, "talk": [ "A flat leather belt runs from a pulley on the engine to a countershaft at the back. A small pinion on that shaft turns a ring gear with its teeth on the inside, fixed to the rear wheel.", "The pulleys are stepped. With the belt on the small engine pulley and the big countershaft pulley the wheel turns slowly but pushes hard; that is low gear. There is no clutch: the belt is moved across by hand, standing still.", "The engine is governed to about 600 rpm, so each gear has one speed: about 12 km/h in high. Half a horsepower moves the rider on the level, but a hill of a few per cent stops it in high gear. Try it with the Hill slider.", "The stepped pulleys are sized so that the same belt fits both steps: what one step loses in diameter on the engine, the other gains on the countershaft." ] }, { "label": "Engine", "params": { "label": "Single cylinder under the seat" }, "talk": [ "The engine is a smaller sister of the Grandfather Clock: one upright cylinder of 264 cm³ (58 × 100 mm), 0.5 hp at 600 rpm, its flywheels closed inside a round crankcase.", "Ignition is by a hot tube kept glowing by a burner, right under the rider. On the first rides the seat is said to have caught fire.", "The intake valve is sucked open by the piston; only the exhaust valve is driven, by a groove cut into the flywheel.", "Fuel came from a surface carburettor behind the seat: air bubbled through petrol and came out as mixture. There was no fuel tank as such." ] } ], "note": "Daimler and Maybach patented the riding car on 29 August 1885 (DRP 36423); the first ride, from Cannstatt to Untertürkheim, followed in November 1885. The original burned in the 1903 Cannstatt factory fire: the museum shows a replica. Sources disagree on who rode first, Paul or Adolf Daimler.", "unverified": [ "Pulley diameters, pinion and ring-gear sizes, and the low-gear speed (about 6 km/h)", "Laden mass (with a 75 kg rider), rolling resistance and driveline efficiency", "Engine position, conrod and crankcase size", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Room: Legend 1 (a museum replica has also been lent to the KTM Motohall)" ] }
=== patent-motorwagen
{ "title": "The first car's engine and differential", "name": "Benz Patent-Motorwagen", "summary": "Karl Benz's three-wheeler: one cylinder lying flat, a horizontal flywheel, and a differential so the rear wheels can turn at different speeds.", "eyebrow": "Benz Patent-Motorwagen, 1886", "lede": "The car Karl Benz patented in January 1886. Watch its single cylinder lying flat at the back, then the differential on the countershaft letting the outer rear wheel run faster in a bend.", "photo": { "alt": "Green-wheeled 1886 Benz Patent-Motorwagen on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the rear left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Engine", "params": { "label": "Single cylinder, lying flat" }, "talk": [ "One cylinder of 954 cm³ (90 × 150 mm) lies flat at the back and gives 0.75 hp at about 400 rpm.", "The big flywheel lies flat as well. Benz did that so its gyroscopic effect would not fight the steering in tight bends.", "Benz used electric ignition with a coil and a spark plug instead of Daimler's hot tube.", "The whole transmission is a belt to a countershaft: one speed and no reverse. Pushing the belt onto a loose pulley next to the drive pulley is the clutch." ] }, { "label": "Differential", "params": { "label": "Differential on the countershaft" }, "talk": [ "In a bend the outer rear wheel has further to go than the inner one. On one solid shaft one of them would have to skid.", "Benz built a differential into the countershaft. The belt turns its housing, and small bevel gears inside let the two output shafts run at different speeds.", "From each end of the countershaft a chain runs to a rear wheel, as on a bicycle.", "Move the steering slider. Straight ahead the small spider gears ride round with the housing without turning; in a bend they start to spin." ] } ], "note": "Patent DRP 37435 of 29 January 1886 is often called the birth certificate of the car. Some sources give 91.4 × 150 mm (984 cm³) for the engine.", "unverified": [ "Wheel sizes, track and wheelbase", "Conrod length, flywheel size, engine and countershaft positions", "Sprocket sizes and the 12 km/h cornering speed", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)" ] }
=== daimler-boat
{ "title": "The engine in the cupboard", "name": "Daimler motorboat Marie", "summary": "A launch with a petrol engine hidden in a white cabinet, so that passengers would not worry about an explosion.", "eyebrow": "Daimler motorboat Marie, 1888", "lede": "Daimler sold the fast-running engine to boat owners before car buyers. Here it sits out of sight behind white doors at the stern, turning a shaft to the propeller. Watch the single cylinder inside the cabinet.", "photo": { "alt": "White, blue and red 1888 Daimler launch Marie with a canopy and a white engine cabinet, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the stern" }, "components": [ { "label": "Engine", "params": { "label": "Single cylinder inside the cabinet" }, "talk": [ "People feared that petrol on board would explode. So the engine hides in a tall white cabinet behind two doors; only the throttle handle and the starting crank stick out. Daimler's first test boat of 1886 even carried insulators and wires so that it looked electric.", "The engine is Daimler's single cylinder with hot-tube ignition: 1.1 hp at 650 rpm, cooled by water from the lake.", "Its crankshaft drives a shaft straight back to a fixed propeller under the stern; the boat is steered with a rudder and tiller. The catalogue describes going astern with a friction cone and a hand lever.", "The 5.5-metre launch made about 5.5 knots, some 10 km/h. Daimler bought the hulls from boatyards and began building motorboats in series in 1888, before cars." ] } ], "note": "Marie is 5.50 m long and 1.45 m wide and weighs 905 kg empty. It belonged to Otto von Bismarck's family and was named after Bismarck's daughter Marie. Its engine is assumed here to be the 462 cm³ (70 × 120 mm) single cylinder of Daimler's 1886 motor carriage, which also gave 1.1 hp at 650 rpm.", "unverified": [ "Bore and stroke (assumed: the 1886 motor-carriage engine), conrod and flywheel size", "Engine position in the hull, cabinet size and hull heights (approximate photo fit)", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Room: Legend 1" ] }

