cylinder positions, wheelbase", "That six were built", "Ghost-body outline" ] }
=== milnes-daimler
{ "title": "Double-declutching through London", "name": "Milnes-Daimler double-decker bus", "summary": "A German Daimler chassis with a London double-deck body, from the first years of the motor bus.", "eyebrow": "Milnes-Daimler double-decker bus, 1907", "lede": "The chassis came from Daimler in Germany; the body was built in England. Its driver changed gear with no synchromesh, all day in city traffic. Try it: a gear only slides in when both wheels' teeth are already moving at the same speed.", "photo": { "alt": "Red 1907 Milnes-Daimler double-decker bus of the London Motor Omnibus Company, with open top deck and advertising boards, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Gearbox", "params": { "label": "Four-speed sliding-gear box" }, "talk": [ "Daimler built the chassis in Germany; Milnes-Daimler sold them in Britain, and British coachbuilders added the double-deck body. In the first years of the motor bus, many of London's buses were Milnes-Daimlers like this one of the London Motor Omnibus Company's Vanguard fleet.", "Like the cars of its day, it has a four-speed sliding-gear box with no synchromesh, behind a cone clutch. The lever slides a pair of gears into mesh, and they only go in when their teeth are already moving at about the same speed.", "To shift up, drivers pressed the clutch and waited for the gearbox to slow. To shift down, they double-declutched: neutral, clutch up, a blip of throttle, clutch down, then into gear. Bus drivers did this all day in traffic.", "\"SPEED 12 M.P.H.\" is painted on the side: about 19 km/h, the legal limit in Britain for a vehicle this heavy. The demo shifts up through all four gears; then try it yourself." ] } ], "note": "Sources date the museum's bus to 1907; similar Milnes-Daimler buses elsewhere are dated 1904. Daimler's commercial chassis of 1907 all had a cone clutch and a four-speed gearbox.", "unverified": [ "Gear teeth, final ratio, engine speeds, gate layout and the drive figures", "Wheelbase (4,000 mm), tracks and wheel radii used for the photo fit", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Year: 1907 (the research notes say 1904)", "Room: Collection 1 (the research notes put it in Legend 2)" ] }
=== ssk
{ "title": "The SSK's switchable supercharger", "name": "Mercedes-Benz SSK", "summary": "A 7.1-litre straight-six with a Roots blower that only cuts in when the driver stamps the pedal through to the floor.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 27/170/225 PS Typ SSK, 1928", "lede": "The short-chassis Super Sport, shown working: the blower that joins in only at full pedal, and the big six-cylinder engine it feeds.", "photo": { "alt": "White 1928 Mercedes-Benz SSK on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Supercharger", "params": { "engineLabel": "7.1-litre straight-six" }, "talk": [ "SSK means Super Sport Kurz, the short-chassis Super Sport. Its full name gives the power: 27 tax horsepower, 170 PS on its own and 225 PS with the blower working.", "The blower is not always on. The driver pushes the pedal to the floor and then a little further; a clutch on the nose of the crankshaft couples the blower in. Lift off and it lets go again. It was meant for short bursts.", "The blower pushes air through the carburettor rather than sucking mixture through itself, the same arrangement the 540 K used a few years later. Watch the air change colour when the clutch bites." ] }, { "label": "Six-cylinder engine", "params": { "label": "Four-stroke cycle · firing order 1-5-3-6-2-4" }, "talk": [ "Six cylinders in line, each 100 mm across and with a 150 mm stroke: 7.1 litres, with one overhead camshaft.", "Firing order 1-5-3-6-2-4 gives a power stroke every 120° of crank, alternating between the front and rear halves of the engine. Watch the orange flashes.", "It was the last Mercedes designed under Ferdinand Porsche before he left the company, and Rudolf Caracciola raced it." ] } ], "note": "Geometry is illustrative: blower layout, drive ratio, boost, conrod length and cylinder spacing are plausible values, not factory data.", "unverified": [ "Blower layout, 2.6 drive ratio and 0.45 bar boost", "Conrod length and cylinder spacing", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Room and level" ] }
=== 260d
{ "title": "The first diesel car", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 260 D", "summary": "The first series-production diesel passenger car: a 2.6-litre four with prechambers and Bosch injection, 45 PS.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 260 D (W 138), 1936", "lede": "A diesel engine has no spark plug: squeezing the air makes it hot enough to light the fuel. Watch fuel spray into each prechamber just before the piston reaches the top, then slide the compression ratio.", "photo": { "alt": "Black 1938 Mercedes-Benz 260 D Pullman saloon on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front right" }, "components": [ { "label": "Engine", "params": { "label": "Diesel four · firing order 1-3-4-2" }, "talk": [ "Shown at the Berlin motor show in February 1936: the first diesel car built in series, often used as a taxi.", "The OM 138 engine: four cylinders, 90 × 100 mm, 2,545 cm³, 45 PS at 3,000 rpm. Few petrol cars went further on a tank.", "Fuel is not injected into the cylinder itself but into a small prechamber in the head. It starts burning there and shoots into the cylinder, which made the diesel smooth and quiet enough for a car.", "Bosch supplied the injection pump. Slide the compression ratio down to petrol values and the air no longer gets hot enough to light the fuel." ] } ], "note": "Legend 3 is the room of diesel and supercharger: the 260 D and the SSK and 540 K blowers are two answers to the same question of more power and range.", "unverified": [ "Compression ratio 20.5 : 1", "Valve gear drawn as pushrod overhead valves", "Conrod length, cylinder spacing, engine position", "Wheelbase and ghost-body outline", "Photo camera fitted from one wheel, the headlamps and the grille only, so the overlay is approximate", "One source gives 3,200 rpm for the rated speed" ] }

