=== 540k
{ "title": "Under the skin of the 540 K", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 540 K (W 29)", "summary": "Independent wishbone front suspension, the Roots blower that only joins in at full pedal, and the streamlined body.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 540 K (W 29), 1936–1940", "lede": "Three things about the supercharged 540 K, each shown working: the independent front suspension meeting a bump and a pothole, the Roots blower that only joins in when the driver floors the pedal, and what a streamlined body does for top speed. Switch to the museum photo to see the first two in place on the real car.", "photo": { "alt": "Black and white 1937 Mercedes-Benz 540 K Cabriolet B on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Front suspension", "params": { "label": "Left front wheel · 25 km/h, shown 5× slower", "links": [ { "name": "Lower wishbone, front leg" }, { "name": "Lower wishbone, rear leg" }, { "name": "Upper wishbone, front leg" }, { "name": "Upper wishbone, rear leg" }, { "name": "Track rod" } ], "legendTitle": "Parts", "legend": [ [ null, "Lower wishbone (two legs)" ], [ null, "Upper wishbone (two legs)" ], [ null, "Track rod" ], [ null, "Coil spring" ], [ null, "Frame and knuckle" ] ] }, "talk": [ "Most cars of the 1930s still had a rigid beam axle on leaf springs at the front. Mercedes gave the 380 of 1933 parallel wishbones and coil springs, and the 500 K and 540 K kept them.", "Each front wheel now moves on its own: a bump under one wheel no longer tilts the other. Watch the orange trace spike over the bump and fall away in the pothole.", "With near-parallel arms the wheel stays almost upright, but the tyre slides sideways a few millimetres as it rises. See the track change readout." ] }, { "label": "Supercharger", "params": { "engineLabel": "5.4-litre straight-eight" }, "talk": [ "K stands for Kompressor. The 5.4-litre straight-eight makes 115 PS on its own and 180 PS with the blower working.", "The blower is not always on. Push the pedal through to the floor and a multi-plate clutch on the nose of the crankshaft couples it in; the engine's normal air inlet closes at the same moment. It was meant for short bursts.", "Mercedes blew the air through the carburettor instead of sucking mixture through the blower. A Roots blower does not squeeze air inside itself: the lobes carry pockets of air round the outside of the casing and push them into the outlet." ] }, { "label": "Air drag", "params": { "label": "Full power on the autobahn", "shapes": [ { "label": "Streamliner (1938)" }, { "label": "Cabriolet B body" } ] }, "talk": [ "In 1938 Mercedes built one 540 K with a closed, streamlined body so that Dunlop could test tyres at sustained high speed on the new autobahns.", "Mercedes-Benz Classic measured the restored car in the wind tunnel in May 2014: a drag coefficient of 0.36, remarkable for its time.", "With the same 180 PS it reaches about 185 km/h, against about 170 km/h for an open Cabriolet. Drag rises with the square of speed, so those 15 km/h take a lot of power: slide it and see." ] } ], "note": "Geometry is illustrative: suspension hardpoints, blower layout, drive ratio, boost and spring rates are plausible values, not factory data.", "unverified": [ "Suspension hardpoints and rates", "Blower layout, 2.6 drive ratio, 0.5 bar boost, carburettor position", "Ghost-body outline", "Whether the Cabriolet B or the 1938 Streamliner is on display", "Cabriolet B drag coefficient 0.48; frontal area, mass, rolling resistance and driveline efficiency of both bodies" ] }
=== l6500
{ "title": "Twelve and a half litres of diesel", "name": "Mercedes-Benz L 6500", "summary": "A heavy truck for 6.5 tonnes with a 12.5-litre six-cylinder prechamber diesel of 150 hp.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz L 6500, 1938", "lede": "By the late 1930s heavy trucks ran on diesel. Watch the OM 57's six cylinders fire with no spark plugs: fuel is sprayed into a small prechamber, and the heat of compression lights it.", "photo": { "alt": "White 1938 Mercedes-Benz L 6500 flatbed truck with a long bonnet in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Diesel engine", "params": { "label": "OM 57 diesel six · firing order 1-5-3-6-2-4" }, "talk": [ "The OM 57 has six cylinders of 125 × 170 mm, 12.5 litres in all, and gives 150 hp at only 1,700 rpm. Its crankshaft runs in seven main bearings, one between every pair of cylinders.", "There are no spark plugs. Squeezing the air heats it far beyond the point where diesel fuel lights; try the compression slider to see why a petrol engine's ratio would not be enough.", "A Bosch pump sprays the fuel into a small prechamber in the head. Burning starts there and shoots into the cylinder, which made these early diesels smoother and less fussy about fuel.", "The camshaft sits low in the block and works the valves through pushrods. A four-speed gearbox with an overdrive and a 6.5-tonne payload: top speed was about 57 km/h." ] } ], "note": "Daimler-Benz built the L 6500 (L 54 series) at Gaggenau from 1935 to 1940. The 1938 revision brought the OM 57 engine and an all-steel cab; earlier trucks had the 120 hp OM 79 or the 150 hp OM 54. Sources disagree on the OM 57's displacement (some give 11.2 litres).", "unverified": [ "Wheelbase (4,600 mm), track and headlamp positions used for the photo fit", "Engine position, cylinder spacing and conrod length", "Compression ratio (18 : 1)", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Room: Collection 2 (the research notes put it in Legend 3)" ] }
=== blue-wonder
{ "title": "The fastest truck of its day", "name": "Mercedes-Benz racing car transporter", "summary": "A one-off transporter with a 300 SL engine that could carry a racing car at 160 km/h.", "eyebrow": "Racing car transporter \"Blue Wonder\", 1955", "lede": "The racing department wanted its cars at the track, or back at the factory, overnight. So it built a truck with a sports-car engine and a low, rounded nose. Compare it with a boxy truck: at these speeds nearly all the power goes into pushing air aside.", "photo": { "alt": "Blue 1955 Mercedes-Benz racing car transporter carrying a silver 300 SLR on its deck, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Air drag", "params": { "label": "Full power on the autobahn", "shapes": [ { "label": "Transporter, empty" }, { "label": "With a racing car" }, { "label": "Boxy truck, same engine" } ] }, "talk": [ "Racing manager Alfred Neubauer asked for it and chief engineer Rudolf Uhlenhaut designed it, on a lengthened 300 S frame. The engine is the 300 SL's three-litre six with petrol injection, here giving 192 hp.", "Air drag grows with the square of speed, and the power to overcome it with the cube. Doubling speed takes about eight times the power, so the last few km/h are the most expensive.", "The low, rounded nose and small frontal area are what make it fast. Switch to the boxy truck: same engine and weight, but a flat face twice the size, and the top speed falls by about 40 km/h.", "A racing car on the open deck adds weight and drag. Even loaded, the transporter was said to reach about 160 km/h." ] } ], "note": "The original was scrapped in 1967; the truck in the museum is a replica rebuilt from photographs and drawings. In the photo it carries a 300 SLR racing sports car. Sources disagree on the engine (some say a 240 hp 300 SL unit).", "unverified": [ "Drag coefficients, frontal areas, masses and driveline efficiency", "The boxy truck is a made-up comparison, not a real vehicle", "Wheelbase (2,900 mm), track and lamp positions used for the photo fit", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Room: Collection 2" ] }
=== transporter-1624
{ "title": "Lifting a deck full of cars", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 1624 car transporter", "summary": "A car transporter whose decks are raised and tilted by one hydraulic pump through cables and pulley blocks.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 1624 car transporter, 1980", "lede": "Four new cars, six tonnes, stacked on two levels. The decks can be raised, lowered and tilted: one central pump makes the power, and steel cables carry it over pulleys to each end of a deck. Try the positions below.", "photo": { "alt": "Blue 1980 Mercedes-Benz 1624 car transporter loaded with four Mercedes cars on two decks, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Deck hoist", "params": { "label": "Upper rear deck · cables and pulley blocks", "presets": [ { "label": "Travel height" }, { "label": "Rear end down: a ramp" }, { "label": "Front end down: nest the cars" }, { "label": "Both ends down" } ] }, "talk": [ "The museum's load: a 230 E and a 280 CE of the 123 series, a 380 SEL and a 500 SEC of the 126 series, about six tonnes of cars. With a trailer, a rig like this carried up to ten.", "The decks have what the museum calls a hybrid drive. One central hydraulic system makes the power, and steel cables carry it to each corner over guide pulleys and pulley blocks.", "Why a pulley block? A short cylinder fits beside the post. Reeve the cable four times between its two blocks, and 30 cm of piston travel becomes 1.2 m of deck travel, for four times the force on the piston.", "Raise both ends to lift the deck, or one end to tilt it: a tilted deck makes a ramp to drive up, or lets one car's bonnet tuck under the next. One pump feeds every cylinder, so moving both ends at once halves the speed.", "The truck itself: a 1624, for 16 tonnes, with a 12.8-litre diesel of 240 hp (177 kW)." ] } ], "note": "Empty, the transporter weighs about 7 tonnes. The museum lifted it into place, five floors above street level, in a 20-tonne crane basket.", "unverified": [ "Hoist layout and sizes: number of falls, cylinder bore and stroke, pump flow, sheave efficiency, deck heights and travel", "Which deck is shown, its mass and the car's position on it", "Wheelbase (5,100 mm), track and lamp positions used for the photo fit", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Year (1980, from the photo caption)", "Room: Collection 2" ] }

