=== c111
{ "title": "Four rotors, no pistons", "name": "Mercedes-Benz C 111-II", "summary": "An orange mid-engined test car with a four-rotor Wankel engine: 350 PS and 300 km/h.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz C 111-II, 1970", "lede": "No pistons, no valves: four triangular rotors turn inside oval housings. Watch one rotor's three chambers go through intake, compression, power and exhaust, all at once and a third of a turn apart.", "photo": { "alt": "Orange 1970 Mercedes-Benz C 111 on a raised display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the rear left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Rotary engine", "params": { "label": "Four-rotor Wankel engine" }, "talk": [ "Felix Wankel's engine has one moving part per chamber group: a curved triangle turning on an eccentric. Its tips seal against a housing shaped like a fat figure eight.", "The rotor turns once for every three turns of the shaft. Each of its three faces makes a power stroke once per rotor turn, so each rotor fires once per shaft turn.", "The C 111-II's M 950 F has four rotors of 600 cm³ per chamber, with a compression ratio of 9.3 : 1 and about 350 PS. Shown at Geneva in March 1970, the car reached 300 km/h.", "Mercedes never built it in series: the Wankel used a lot of fuel, and its apex seals wore. From late 1970 the C 111 ran with diesel and V8 engines instead." ] } ], "note": "Sources disagree on whether to rate the engine as 2.4 or 4.8 litres, and some give 350 PS at 7,000 rpm. The model's compression ratio comes from the rotor shape drawn here.", "unverified": [ "Flank shape (chosen to give 9.3 : 1)", "Rotor phasing and spacing", "Engine position and ghost-body outline", "Rated speed of 7,000 rpm" ] }
=== 190sl
{ "title": "The other SL", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 190 SL", "summary": "The 300 SL's affordable sister: the same look, a saloon floorpan and a new 1.9-litre overhead-camshaft four.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 190 SL, 1959", "lede": "It looks like a 300 SL, but underneath it is far simpler. Its engine is a four-cylinder with the 300 SL's bore and a camshaft in the head, turned by a chain. Watch the cam open each valve directly as the four cylinders fire in turn.", "photo": { "alt": "Grey 1958 Mercedes-Benz 190 SL roadster with red seats and the top down, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Engine", "params": { "label": "M 121 four, overhead camshaft · firing order 1-3-4-2" }, "talk": [ "The 190 SL shares the 300 SL's styling and its suspension layout, wishbones at the front and a swing axle at the rear. But instead of a tubular space frame it sits on a shortened floorpan from the saloons, which made it far cheaper to build.", "Its engine, the M 121, was new: four cylinders with the 300 SL's 85 mm bore and an 83.6 mm stroke, 1.9 litres in all, giving 105 hp at 5,700 rpm through two twin-choke Solex carburettors.", "One camshaft in the head, driven by a double roller chain, opens all eight valves. With no pushrods in between, the valve gear stays light and can follow the cam at high revs.", "Firing order 1-3-4-2: one power stroke every half turn. Pistons 1 and 4 rise and fall together, 2 and 3 opposite them. Detuned, the same engine later powered the 180 and 190 saloons." ] } ], "note": "Since 2017 the museum's 190 SL has been an unrestored 1959 roadster that belonged to astronaut David Scott, the seventh man on the Moon, who bought it new and kept it until 2004. The photo, from 2013, shows the car displayed before it.", "unverified": [ "Engine position in the car and conrod length", "Lamp and star positions used for the photo fit", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the data-sheet sizes and the photo)", "Room: Collection 4 (the research notes put it in Legend 4)" ] }
=== papamobil
{ "title": "Why one wheel spins", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 230 G Popemobile", "summary": "The G-Class built for Pope John Paul II's visit to Germany, and the differential lock that gets a G out of a slippery spot.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 230 G 'Papamobil', 1980", "lede": "Put one rear wheel on ice. With an open differential that wheel spins and the car does not move. Lock the differential and the wheel on dry ground pulls the car away.", "photo": { "alt": "White 1980 Mercedes-Benz 230 G Popemobile with its glass cabin, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Differential lock", "params": { "label": "Rear axle, one wheel on ice" }, "talk": [ "Pearl white with gold trim and a transparent rear superstructure, this 230 G was lent to the Vatican for Pope John Paul II's visit to Germany in 1980. From 1981 it had bullet-proof glass.", "Underneath it is a G-Class, the off-road vehicle built with Steyr-Daimler-Puch from 1979, with four-wheel drive engaged by the driver.", "An open differential always splits the drive equally between its two wheels. If one wheel is on ice it can only take a little, so the other gets just as little, and the car stays where it is while the ice wheel spins.", "A differential lock joins both half-shafts solid. Now the wheel with grip can take most of the drive. The G could have locks on both axles, standard from 1985." ] } ], "note": "Whether this car has differential locks is not known: they were optional on the early G. The Popemobile is shown in the Gallery of Celebrities (Collection 4).", "unverified": [ "Wheelbase (long or short G)", "Axle torque, wheel loads, friction values and the force needed to move off", "Whether this car has differential locks", "Ghost-body outline" ] }

