{ "title": "Under the skin of the 190", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 190 E (W201)", "summary": "Five-link rear axle meeting a bump and a pothole, and the M102 four-cylinder turning over.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 190 E (W201), 1984", "lede": "Two mechanisms of the same car, each shown working: the five-link rear axle meeting a bump and a pothole, and the M102 four-cylinder turning over. Switch to the museum photo to see them in place on the real car.", "photo": { "alt": "White 1984 Mercedes-Benz 190 E on display in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Five-link rear axle", "params": { "label": "Left rear wheel · 25 km/h, shown 5× slower", "links": [ { "name": "Camber strut" }, { "name": "Pull strut" }, { "name": "Push strut" }, { "name": "Spring link" }, { "name": "Track rod" } ], "legendTitle": "The five links" }, "talk": [ "Five rods, each loaded mostly along its own axis, locate the wheel carrier. The 190 was the first series car with this axle, in 1982.", "Watch the orange trace. Over the bump tyre load spikes; in the pothole it drops toward zero. Grip comes from that load, so a flat line is the goal.", "The body barely moves while the wheel does the work. That is the sprung versus unsprung mass split." ] }, { "label": "M102 engine", "params": { "label": "Four-stroke cycle · firing order 1-3-4-2" }, "talk": [ "The 190 E's 2.0-litre M102: four cylinders in line, one overhead camshaft, cylinder 1 at the front.", "Firing order 1-3-4-2 gives one power stroke every 180° of crank, which keeps the crank torque even. Watch the orange flashes walk along the block.", "The block leans to the right so it fits under the 190's low bonnet line." ] } ], "note": "Geometry is illustrative: link hardpoints, engine dimensions and suspension rates are plausible values, not factory data.", "unverified": [ "All suspension hardpoints and spring, damper and tyre rates", "M102 conrod length and 15° block lean", "Ghost-body outline" ] }
=== w196r
{ "title": "Wheels in, or wheels out", "name": "Mercedes-Benz W 196 R streamliner", "summary": "The Formula 1 car built with two bodies: enclosed wheels for fast circuits, open wheels for twisty ones.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz W 196 R streamlined racing car, 1955", "lede": "Mercedes planned two bodies for its 1954 Grand Prix car. Covering the wheels cuts drag on long straights; leaving them open saves weight and lets the driver place the car in corners. Switch bodies and see what the cover is worth at full power.", "photo": { "alt": "Silver 1955 Mercedes-Benz W 196 R streamlined racing car with enclosed wheels and start number 18, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "Air drag", "params": { "label": "Full power on the Monza straight", "shapes": [ { "label": "Streamlined body" }, { "label": "Open wheels" } ] }, "talk": [ "For the new 2.5-litre Formula 1 of 1954, Mercedes planned two bodies from the start: this streamlined one for very fast circuits, and a classic one with open wheels.", "A spinning, exposed wheel churns the air and leaves a big wake. Covering the wheels makes the body wider, so it pushes more air aside, yet the total drag still falls. Switch bodies: at the same 290 hp the streamliner is about 20 km/h faster in this model.", "It won first time out, at Reims on 4 July 1954: Fangio ahead of Kling. Two weeks later, on the tight Silverstone circuit, the drivers could not place the enclosed front wheels precisely in the corners and finished only 4th and 7th.", "In 1955 the streamliner raced only once, at Monza with its new steep banking, where Fangio won. The M 196 engine, a straight eight with direct injection and desmodromic valves, now gave 290 hp at 8,500 rpm." ] } ], "note": "The streamliner ran with the regular 2,350 mm wheelbase; a 2,210 mm version was tried at Monza in 1955 but not raced. Fangio became world champion in both seasons.", "unverified": [ "Drag coefficients, frontal areas, masses and driveline efficiency (no top speed is quoted, so none is compared)", "Tracks, wheel radius and nose position used for the photo fit (approximate: the wheels give a nearly straight line of points)", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo)", "Room: Legend 7" ] }
=== 500slc
{ "title": "A V8 on the rally stages", "name": "Mercedes-Benz 500 SLC rally car", "summary": "A big luxury coupé turned rally winner, with a five-litre aluminium V8.", "eyebrow": "Mercedes-Benz 500 SLC rally car, 1980", "lede": "Eight cylinders in two banks of four, set at 90°. Watch how each pair of opposite cylinders shares one crankpin, and how the firing jumps from bank to bank, not always in turn.", "photo": { "alt": "Silver and black 1980 Mercedes-Benz 500 SLC rally car with four auxiliary lamps and start number 8, in the Mercedes-Benz Museum, seen from the front left" }, "components": [ { "label": "V8 engine", "params": { "label": "M 117 V8, 90° · firing order 1-5-4-8-6-3-7-2" }, "talk": [ "Mercedes went rallying with its big SLC coupés at the end of the 1970s: the 30,000 km Vuelta a la América del Sud, the Safari Rally and the Bandama Rally in Ivory Coast. Björn Waldegård and Hans Thorszelius, whose names this car carries, won the Bandama in 1980.", "The M 117 has two banks of four cylinders at 90°: cylinders 1 to 4 on the right, 5 to 8 on the left. Opposite cylinders share a crankpin, their conrods side by side, so eight cylinders need only four pins and five main bearings.", "The four pins stand 90° apart, a cross-plane crank, so a cylinder fires every 90° of crank. Follow the order 1-5-4-8-6-3-7-2: right, left, right, left, then left twice and right twice. Each bank fires unevenly, which gives the V8 its burble.", "The five-litre version, 96.5 × 85 mm, has an aluminium block with no cylinder liners: the bores are etched so that hard silicon crystals carry the iron-coated pistons. One overhead camshaft per bank works the valves through finger followers. In the road car it gave 240 hp at 4,750 rpm." ] } ], "note": "The 450 SLC 5.0 of 1978, renamed 500 SLC in 1980, had an aluminium bonnet and boot lid to save weight, partly with rallying in mind. The rally engines were tuned beyond the road car's output.", "unverified": [ "Engine position, cylinder spacing, bank offset and conrod length", "Wheel radius (300 mm) and lamp positions used for the photo fit", "Ghost-body outline (traced from the photo and the production car's size)", "Whether this car is the 1980 Bandama winner or a sister car", "Room: Legend 7" ] }

